description:
# Build verify-content.ts and the audit scripts for readings, shelf, misconceptions, dimensions, and instruments

**Epic:** `am-ep-content-model-9e3` · **Batch:** B · **Priority:** P1 · **Plan sections:** §6.2, §7.6, §10.1, §10.7, §11.7, §17.3, §17.7 (traceability only)

## Background

The plan's pipeline (§17.3) names one content gate and five audits:
- `verify-content.ts`: "every compiler rejection of §11.7 plus Rules 0–2";
- `audit-readings.ts`: R0–R3 present; a heuristic R2-length gate with recorded overrides;
- `audit-shelf.ts`: no premise after 1904 in a discovery step unless flagged;
- `audit-misconceptions.ts`: at least five per paper; anchors and instruments resolve;
- `audit-dimensions.ts`: rational-exponent dimensional consistency of every equation;
- `audit-instruments.ts`: registry entry, probes, `notModeled`, tape identity, kernel test, action contract, predict mode or exemption.

The per-paper definition of done requires `verify-content` to be green (§17.7 item 9). The launch readiness audit relies on these reports being honest and separate, never a single score.

Checks are contributed by many beads over many batches: structural, semantic, epistemic, manifest, dimension, review records, voice, anachronism, and show-the-code's kernel-identifier rule. A gate that silently lacks a check is worse than no gate, because it reports green. `verify-content.ts` therefore runs every registered check and proves, against a committed inventory, that none is missing.

## Scope

In scope: the orchestrating `scripts/verify-content.ts`; the registered-check inventory and its test; the four audit scripts not built elsewhere; the Rule 1 pinned-asset check; the revision check invocation; report formats; registration in the quality gate chain.

Out of scope, owned elsewhere:
- the underlying checks (`am-cm-checks-structural-lq0`, `am-cm-checks-semantic-lic`, `am-cm-checks-epistemic-o7n`, `am-cm-source-manifest-6qa`, `am-edit-review-records-hofz`, `am-edit-voice-lint-trmf`, `am-cm-anachronism-lint-dl2`, and the kernel-identifier rule of `am-inst-show-the-code-4brv`);
- `audit-dimensions.ts` itself (`am-cm-dimension-validator-aoz`, orchestrated here);
- the architecture gate (`am-scaf-architecture-gate-l1p`, invoked here);
- `scripts/check-revisions.ts` (`am-cm-id-scheme-8bn`, invoked here).

## Requirements

1. **`verify-content.ts`** runs, in order:
   - the architecture gate (Rule 2);
   - the content compiler with every registered check;
   - `audit-dimensions.ts`;
   - the four audits below;
   - `check-revisions.ts --base <ref>`, where `<ref>` is the CI base ref or `--base` from the command line, and is skipped with an explicit `revision-check-skipped` line only when no base ref exists (a fresh clone with no history);
   - the pinned-asset check.

   It exits non-zero on any error, and prints and logs flags separately from errors.

   **Rule 1 support.** The `pinned-asset-present` check reuses the receipt checker of `am-src-receipt-format-npo5` for receipt-referenced files and applies the same rules to `SourceAsset` records and retained release manifests:
   - a missing file whose `publicationDecision` is `publish` (served from `public/`) is an error naming the asset id and path, so accidental deletions surface immediately;
   - a missing `pin-local-only` file, or a missing retained parent scan, is reported as `not-available` and is neither a pass nor an error. Such files live in the gitignored `sources/` directory, so they are absent on CI and on fresh clones. With `--require-local` the same condition is an error;
   - a `reference-only` asset has no file to check.

   **Rule 0** (the user's override) is not machine-checkable, and the script says so in its help text rather than pretending.
2. **Registered-check inventory.** `scripts/verify-content.checks.json` lists every check id and family that must be registered. `verify-content.ts` compares it with `listRegisteredChecks()` from the compiler:
   - a check that is registered but not in the inventory is an error (`check-not-inventoried`);
   - an inventoried check that is not registered is an error (`check-missing`).

   Each bead that adds a check adds its line to the inventory in the same change, so a gate can never quietly lose a family. The inventory starts with the families that exist when this bead lands and is extended by later beads (anachronism, voice, review records, kernel identifiers).
3. **`audit-readings.ts`:**
   - every paragraph, displayed equation, and instrument caption has R0, R1, R2, and R3;
   - R0 is one or two sentences, counted with `Intl.Segmenter` sentence granularity and an abbreviation list (`Ann. Phys.`, `e.g.`, `i.e.`, `cf.`, `vol.`, `pp.`) so citations do not split sentences;
   - R3 carries at least one citation;
   - the R2 heuristic requires R2 to be longer than its R1 by a configured factor (initially 1.2, measured in words with `Intl.Segmenter` word granularity) unless an override record states the reason and the reviewer.

   Output is per paper and section.
4. **`audit-shelf.ts`:** per journey, lists every shelf card with date, `latestYear`, status, verification state, and source. It fails on undated or unsourced cards and on any post-1904 premise used in a discovery step without the parallel-work or admitted-import flag, reusing the epistemic check's result rather than re-implementing the rule. Later-evidence citations in world-check stages are listed separately as evidence, not premises.
5. **`audit-misconceptions.ts`:** each paper has at least five entries once the ledger is declared or the paper is complete; every anchor, instrument id, result id, and source resolves.
6. **`audit-instruments.ts`:** for every registered instrument (the 33 core rows and the shelf instruments) it reports:
   - registry entry and explicit dispatcher case;
   - membership of core ids in the core catalogue;
   - probes;
   - non-empty `notModeled`;
   - tape model identity;
   - an owner test file that exercises the owner function;
   - an action contract for every interactive action (`am-a11y-action-contracts-k75g`);
   - predict mode enabled or an exemption reason;
   - embed flag;
   - required scenario kinds present;
   - show-the-code kernel functions declared and pinned (once `am-inst-show-the-code-4brv` registers its data).

   The report is a table with one column per requirement and **no aggregate score**. It reads the registry and dispatcher from `am-inst-registry-dispatcher-66l0`, so the audit tests real code paths rather than a fixture list of names.
7. **Registration:** `verify-content` becomes a required step in the quality gate chain (`am-scaf-quality-gates-ci-4xx`); the audits can also run individually.

## Technical Approach

Scripts live in `scripts/`; shared report utilities live in `src/content/audits/`. Each audit exposes a pure function over the compiled corpus (and, for instruments, the registry module) plus a CLI. Override records for the R2 heuristic live in `content/editorial/overrides.yaml` with `targetId`, `rule`, `reason`, `reviewer`, and `date`. Reports are written as JSON to `artifacts/audits/<audit>/<tool-run-id>.json` and as Markdown tables beside them.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] `bun scripts/verify-content.ts` runs every check and audit in order and exits non-zero on errors.
- [ ] A registered but uninventoried check and an inventoried but unregistered check both fail with their ids.
- [ ] Each audit has fixture corpora producing expected pass and fail reports.
- [ ] The R2 override path requires a reason and reviewer; an override without them fails.
- [ ] R0 sentence counting does not split on the listed abbreviations.
- [ ] A missing pinned asset fails `verify-content` with the asset id and path.
- [ ] `audit-instruments.ts` output has per-requirement columns and no total, and it reads the real registry and dispatcher.
- [ ] The revision check runs against the base ref in CI and is reported as skipped only when no base exists.
- [ ] `verify-content` is a required step in the gate chain.

## Test Plan

### Unit and integration tests
- `auditReadings.test.ts`: a paragraph missing R3 fails; an R2 shorter than R1 without override fails; the same with a valid override passes; an override without a reviewer fails; a three-sentence R0 fails; a two-sentence R0 containing "Ann. Phys. 17, p. 549" passes.
- `auditShelf.test.ts`: an undated card fails; a 1905 premise without flag fails; a Millikan 1916 dataset cited as world-check evidence is listed as evidence and does not fail.
- `auditMisconceptions.test.ts`: four entries on a complete paper fail; a dangling instrument id fails; a dangling result id fails.
- `auditInstruments.test.ts`: a fixture instrument registered through the real registry, missing its dispatcher case and predict exemption, fails on exactly those columns; `bm-09` fails catalogue membership.
- `checkInventory.test.ts`: the three inventory states (match, uninventoried, missing).
- `verifyContent.test.ts`: exit codes for clean and failing fixture corpora; a missing pinned asset fixture; the help text states that Rule 0 cannot be machine-checked; the revision check is skipped with a logged line when no base ref exists.

### End-to-end tests
- The quality gate chain runs `bun scripts/verify-content.ts` on the real corpus on every push, and on the fixture corpora in its own test job with asserted exit codes and report contents.
- A deliberately broken fixture branch (one error from each check family) must produce one error line per family in a single run, which proves that no family stops the others.

### Structured logging
- Each script writes `{timestamp, suite, runId, beadId: "am-cm-audit-scripts-d34", check, family, paper?, recordId?, requirement?, expected?, actual?, outcome, message}` lines through the shared logger to `artifacts/test-logs/content/<log-run-id>.jsonl`.
- `verify-content` writes a summary line with error and flag counts per family and the list of skipped steps with reasons.
- Failing audits retain their JSON and Markdown reports under `artifacts/audits/`.

## Considerations and Pitfalls

- A length heuristic cannot judge an explanation; it only catches an R2 that obviously did not expand R1. The R2 readability review remains required.
- Keep audits fast enough to run on every push; otherwise they get skipped.
- Never introduce a "completeness score"; the plan forbids aggregating unlike dimensions.
- A green gate that silently lacks a check family is the most dangerous failure here; the inventory test exists to make that impossible.

## Dependencies

Blocked by:
- the checks to orchestrate: `am-cm-checks-structural-lq0`, `am-cm-checks-semantic-lic`, `am-cm-checks-epistemic-o7n`, `am-cm-dimension-validator-aoz`;
- `am-scaf-architecture-gate-l1p` (Rule 2);
- `am-scaf-quality-gates-ci-4xx` (registration in the chain).

Proposed in this review round: `am-cm-source-manifest-6qa` (the manifest check runs inside `verify-content`) and `am-inst-registry-dispatcher-66l0` (the instrument audit reads the real registry).

Unblocks: `am-inst-show-the-code-4brv` (registers the kernel-identifier check into `verify-content.ts`), `am-launch-readiness-audit-sc9b` (reads these reports).

# Implement the structural compiler rejections

**Epic:** `am-ep-content-model-9e3` · **Batch:** A · **Priority:** P0 · **Plan sections:** §3.1, §4.4, §6.9, §11.7, §17.3 (traceability only)

## Background

The plan lists what the first compiler must reject (§11.7). The structural subset catches broken references and impossible records before any reader sees them:
- duplicate ids, missing source blocks, broken alignment edges, dangling citations;
- impossible date ordering;
- an English equation block that is not byte-identical to its aligned German block;
- a paper marked complete while required blocks or explanation obligations are absent;
- a hero quote that does not resolve to edition text at its anchor.

These failures are cheap to detect and expensive to discover in production. That is especially true in a bilingual edition, where a single broken alignment edge silently detaches a translation from its source.

## Scope

In scope: registering the structural checks with the compiler, their error messages, and fixtures.

Out of scope, owned elsewhere: symbol, quantity, owner, and math-command checks (`am-cm-checks-semantic-lic`); proof, shelf, and accessibility checks (`am-cm-checks-epistemic-o7n`); manifest completeness, locators, references, frozen ids, and result imports (`am-cm-source-manifest-6qa`); the German-edition-contained-in-ledger test, math token sequence matching, and gloss token checks (`am-edn-alignment-tooling-do1`); review-record coverage of `reviewed` states (`am-edit-review-records-hofz`).

## Requirements

Each check reports `{recordId, file, path, rule, message, repair}` through the compiler's check API:

1. `duplicate-id`: two records share an id within a namespace (source ids within one document of one paper; generic record ids within an entity namespace; equation record ids and argument ids globally).
2. `missing-source-block`: a paper's ordered block list, a translation unit, an alignment edge, an editorial note, or an argument node's source support names a block or sentence that does not exist. Cross-paper `{paper, id}` references are resolved against the named paper.
3. `broken-alignment`:
   - an edge's source sentence is not inside its block;
   - an edge's target translation unit is missing;
   - a character range is out of bounds;
   - a German sentence (paragraph, heading, masthead, footnote, or closing sentence) has no alignment edge;
   - an English translation unit has no alignment edge;
   - a suffixed translation unit (`s3-p2-s1a`) has no sibling (a split needs at least two parts), or an unsuffixed translation unit exists beside suffixed ones for the same source sentence.
4. `dangling-citation`: a citation id is referenced by a `reference` inline, an `EditorialNote.sourceSupport`, a card, an argument node's evidence, or a dataset, but not defined.
5. `impossible-date-order`, precision-aware. Each typed date is an interval (a day is one day; `1905-05` is 1–31 May 1905; `1905` is the whole year). A constraint "X is not after Y" fails only when the earliest possible X is later than the latest possible Y. Checked pairs:
   - date-line not after received;
   - received not after issue publication;
   - issue publication not after any later edition;
   - date-line not after submitted.

   So "Bern, Mai 1905" with receipt on 11 May 1905 is consistent, and the dissertation's real dates (dated 30 April 1905, submitted 20 July 1905) pass. A fixture with a date-line of "Bern, Juni 1905" and receipt on 11 May 1905 fails, as does a fixture that swaps the dissertation's date-line and submission date.
6. `equation-not-identical`: an English-face equation block whose serialized mathematical content differs by even one byte from the aligned German equation block. Equations are shared by id wherever possible; any duplicated block must match exactly, with no whitespace or Unicode normalization.
7. `complete-while-missing`: a paper or section marked complete while the source manifest reports required units without reviewed statuses, or while argument nodes in that scope lack coverage obligations.
8. `hero-quote-unresolved`: a hero or pull quote's exact text is not found in the edition text of the declared language at its anchor. Matching collapses runs of whitespace to one space and nothing else: no case folding, and no normalization of quotation marks, dashes, or ligatures. A quote containing an ellipsis must be authored as ordered `segments[]`, each of which must be found in order within the anchor's text.
9. `ledger-marker-in-edition`: edition text contains `--- REVIEWED TRANSCRIPTION PAGE` (the ledger marker pattern `--- REVIEWED TRANSCRIPTION PAGE <n> OF <N> ---`) or any other scan-page furniture recorded for that document by the ledger validator. Page locators belong in the ledger and receipt.

## Technical Approach

Implement each rule as a registered check in `src/content/checks/structural/` using the compiler's indexes. Date comparison uses the typed date schema from `am-cm-schemas-source-1en` with interval semantics in `src/content/checks/structural/dateIntervals.ts`, which `am-cm-source-manifest-6qa` also uses for import chronology. Each rule is independent, so one broken record never hides another failure.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] All nine rules are registered and produce errors that fail the build.
- [ ] Each rule has at least one failing fixture and one passing fixture.
- [ ] Messages name the record id, the field path, and a concrete repair.
- [ ] Month-precision comparisons pass "Mai 1905 / 11 May" and fail a genuinely inverted order; the dissertation's real dates pass and the swapped fixture fails.
- [ ] Heading, footnote-sentence, masthead, and closing units are covered by the alignment rule.
- [ ] The real (initially empty) corpus passes.

## Test Plan

### Unit and integration tests
`src/content/checks/structural/structural.test.ts`: a base valid fixture corpus plus one mutation per rule, run through the real compiler. Each case asserts the rule id and record id in the error:
- a duplicate paragraph id;
- a translation unit pointing to `s9-p1`, which does not exist;
- a cross-paper reference to `special-relativity#eq-s8-d9`, which does not exist;
- an alignment edge whose range extends beyond the sentence;
- an unaligned German sentence, an unaligned footnote sentence, and an unaligned section heading;
- a lone suffixed unit `s2-p1-s3a`;
- the citation `pais-1982` referenced by an editorial note but undefined;
- a received date `1905-03-16` before the date-line `1905-03-17`;
- "Bern, Juni 1905" with receipt on 11 May 1905;
- the dissertation fixture with swapped date-line and submission;
- an English equation block with an extra space;
- a paper marked complete with a machine-draft translation;
- a hero quote with one changed word, and an ellipsis quote whose segments appear out of order;
- an edition paragraph containing a ledger page marker.

`src/content/checks/structural/dateIntervals.test.ts`: day, month, and year intervals; boundary equality (the same day passes); leap-year months; a year-precision date against a day-precision date.

### End-to-end tests
The quality gate chain runs `bun scripts/build-content.ts --corpus <fixture-mutation>` for every mutation through a test-only entry point and asserts a non-zero exit with the expected rule ids in the JSONL log; the base corpus must exit zero.

### Structured logging
- Findings: `{timestamp, suite: "content-structural", runId, beadId: "am-cm-checks-structural-lq0", rule, recordId, file, path, expected, actual, message, repair}` lines in `artifacts/test-logs/content/<log-run-id>.jsonl` through the shared logger.
- Unit suites log `testId`, `mutation`, `expectedRule`, `actualRules`, `outcome`, and `durationMs`.
- A failing mutation test retains the mutated fixture path in its log line.

## Considerations and Pitfalls

- "Byte-identical" is deliberately strict; it prevents a translation workflow from quietly "tidying" a formula.
- Date precision matters: many date-lines give only a month. Compare intervals, never truncated strings.
- Keep checks independent so one broken record does not hide other failures.
- Hero-quote matching must not be forgiving; a curly quote silently replaced by a straight one is still a misquotation.

## Dependencies

Blocked by: `am-cm-compiler-core-oa7` (check API and indexes).

Unblocks: `am-edn-alignment-tooling-do1` (builds on alignment rules), `am-cm-audit-scripts-d34` (includes these checks in `verify-content.ts`).

# Implement the source manifest compiler and locator validator

**Epic:** `am-ep-content-model-9e3` · **Batch:** A · **Priority:** P0 · **Plan sections:** §1.5, §3.2, §3.3–3.7, §4.3, §5.1, §19.1, §19.4 (traceability only)

## Background

"Complete papers" is the first non-negotiable outcome: every original paragraph, displayed equation, substantive inline equation, footnote, qualification, date-line, acknowledgment, and reference has a place in the edition, including the difficult closing sections popular accounts skip (paper 3 §§6–10, paper 1 §9) (§1.5). The plan makes completeness measurable: before writing explanations, inventory each document into stable blocks with locators and per-layer statuses. **The manifest, not a hand-maintained percentage, determines whether an edition is complete**, and a block cannot be "covered" by linking to a whole paper (§3.2). This is the first concrete work item in the plan (§19.4), "Source manifest compiler: canonical source units, alignment graph, locator validator," closed when "missing or duplicate original units produce an actionable failure." Batch A's exit evidence depends on it: "every original source unit has a destination" (§19.1).

Printed pages do not map neatly onto units. Paragraphs cross page breaks, a page can sit entirely inside one long paragraph, and a footnote can be printed on the page after its mark. PDF page indices can differ from journal pages when covers or editorial matter are included. The locator rules below are written for those realities, so a correct inventory never fails a naive rule.

The companion record is deliberately scoped. It covers what the Avogadro lab needs, and it must never imply that the whole dissertation is edited, so the manifest records out-of-scope units explicitly.

## Scope

In scope: the manifest file format, the manifest compiler check, the locator validator, reference and footnote completeness, frozen-id validation wiring, cross-paper result exports and imports, completeness and status reports per paper.

Out of scope, owned elsewhere: authoring each paper's manifest (`am-edn-inventory-light-quanta-skp`, `am-edn-inventory-brownian-slg`, `am-edn-inventory-relativity-0u9`, `am-edn-inventory-mass-energy-g2d`; the companion's scoped manifest through `am-src-ledger-dissertation-ppm7` and `am-companion-dissertation-record-nbvv`); alignment tooling (`am-edn-alignment-tooling-do1`); argument-level coverage (`am-cm-coverage-ledger-0ip`); the id grammar and frozen-id helper (`am-cm-id-scheme-8bn`).

## Requirements

1. **Format:** `content/source-blocks/<slug>/manifest.yaml`.
   - **Header:** `paper`, `documents[]` (the primary `bibKey` and any related document such as `ap-34-591`), `frozen` (`date` and the snapshot path `manifest.ids.snapshot.txt`, both written by the inventory bead at freeze), `figures: none` (the four papers and the scoped companion contain no source figures; the field is explicit), and optional `exportedResults` and `importedResults`.
   - **Body:** an ordered list of every source unit.
2. **Unit kinds** map one-to-one to `SourceBlock` kinds and the id grammar:
   - `masthead-title` and `masthead-author` (block kind `masthead`);
   - `part-heading` (`part-<k>`, block kind `heading`; paper 3 has two parts) and `section-heading` (`s<n>-h`);
   - `paragraph` (`s<n>-p<m>`, with its sentence ids listed);
   - `display-equation` (`eq-…`);
   - `inline-equation` (`s<n>-p<m>-s<k>-m<i>` or the footnote form), listed under the unit that contains it;
   - `footnote` (`s<n>-fn<k>`, with its sentence ids);
   - `closing-dateline` and `closing-ack`.
3. **References are units too.** Each unit lists its printed references as `references[]`: `{sentenceId, kind: internal | external, targetId? | citationId?}`. Internal references name a section, equation, or footnote id. External references name a `Citation` id with role `primary` or `technical`. No printed reference may be dropped, including the relativity paper's footnotes and the mass–energy paper's citation of the relativity paper.
4. **Per unit:** `id` (per `am-cm-id-scheme-8bn`), `kind`, `document` (when not the primary document), `locator` (`start` and optional `end`, each `{pdfPageIndex, printedPage, region?}`), `originalLabel`, `editorialLabel`, `scope` (`in-scope`, or `not-in-scope` with a `reason`; only the companion may use `not-in-scope`), and derived statuses for `transcription`, `mathTranscription`, `translation`, and `review`.
5. **Statuses are computed, not asserted.** A unit's translation status comes from the existence and review state of its `TranslationUnit` records and alignment edges; its transcription statuses come from its `SourceBlock`. A manifest line that asserts a status is rejected (`status-asserted`); the report is the only place statuses appear.
6. **Locator validation:**
   - `pdfPageIndex` lies within the pinned `SourceAsset.pageCount` of the unit's document;
   - `printedPage` lies within that document's journal range (paper 1: 132–148; paper 2: 549–560; paper 3: 891–921; paper 4: 639–641; dissertation: 289–306; correction: 591–592) and agrees with the asset's page mapping (PDF page index, printed journal page, and any translation page are different things);
   - `region` values are percentages from 0 to 100, and `end` never precedes `start`;
   - body units (everything except footnotes) never move backward in printed page along manifest order, comparing each unit's `start` with the previous body unit's `end` (or `start` when it has no `end`);
   - a footnote's `start` page equals the page of the sentence that carries its mark, or the following page when the printed footnote begins there, and the manifest records which;
   - every printed page of an in-scope document range is covered by at least one unit span, so a page lying entirely inside one paragraph passes (for the companion, pages that hold only `not-in-scope` units are covered by those units).
7. **Completeness failures with actionable messages:**
   - duplicate unit ids;
   - an edition block, translation unit, or note referring to a unit missing from the manifest;
   - an in-scope manifest unit with no destination record (the Batch A exit rule); a `not-in-scope` unit is listed with its reason and needs no destination, so the scoped companion manifest can list every out-of-scope dissertation block without failing;
   - a paragraph sequence gap (`s2-p1`, `s2-p3` without `s2-p2`), and likewise for sentences within a paragraph and footnotes within a section;
   - a repeated printed equation number that did not use the section-qualified id;
   - a `footnote-mark` inline pointing to a missing footnote, or a footnote with no mark (unless the unit records `unmarked: true` with a reason);
   - a paper marked complete while any required unit lacks reviewed statuses;
   - a companion that claims whole-document completeness while any unit is `not-in-scope`;
   - ids that break the frozen snapshot, checked with `validateFrozenIds` from `am-cm-id-scheme-8bn` (a missing frozen id without an alias, or a reused retired id).
8. **Exported and imported results.** A paper's manifest header may declare `exportedResults`: stable ids of results that another paper imports with provenance (`{resultId, blockIds, equationIds}`). The relativity manifest exports its §8 light-energy transformation ($E'/E = \beta(1 - \frac{v}{V}\cos\varphi)$ in modern-factor form; the exact printed form, explicit root or $\beta$, is recorded from the facsimile) so the mass–energy argument can cite it by id. The importing manifest declares `importedResults` (`{fromPaper, resultId}`).
   - The compiler rejects an import that names an id not listed in the source paper's `exportedResults`.
   - It rejects a paper that imports its own conclusion (an import whose result id resolves to the importing paper).
   - It rejects an import from a paper whose `received` date is not earlier than the importing paper's `received` date, compared with the precision-aware interval rule of `am-cm-checks-structural-lq0`. The relativity paper was received 30 June 1905 and the mass–energy paper 27 September 1905, so the real import passes.
9. **Reports:** `bun scripts/source-manifest-report.ts <slug> [--json]` prints per-status counts by kind and the list of incomplete units. The JSON form is written to `artifacts/source-manifest/<slug>-<tool-run-id>.json` and feeds `am-cm-coverage-ledger-0ip`. No single aggregated percentage is printed, and `not-in-scope` counts are shown separately from in-scope counts.

## Technical Approach

Implement as a registered compiler check plus a report CLI in `src/content/manifest/`. Read page counts and page mappings from `SourceAsset` records; compute statuses from translation, alignment, and review records; call the id scheme's parsers and `validateFrozenIds` rather than re-implementing them.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] The manifest format is documented in `content/README.md` and validated by schema.
- [ ] Each failure class in requirement 7, each locator rule in requirement 6, and each import rule in requirement 8 has a fixture that produces an actionable error naming unit id, rule, and repair.
- [ ] Statuses are derived from records; a fixture asserting `reviewed` in the manifest fails with `status-asserted`.
- [ ] A paragraph that spans three printed pages covers the middle page; a footnote printed on the page after its mark passes when recorded as such.
- [ ] The report CLI prints per-dimension counts without an aggregate percentage and writes its JSON form.
- [ ] The empty corpus passes (no papers declared) and a declared paper with an empty manifest fails.
- [ ] A scoped companion fixture with `not-in-scope` units passes, and the same fixture claiming whole-document completeness fails.

## Test Plan

### Unit and integration tests
`src/content/manifest/manifest.test.ts`, running the real check on fixture corpora:
- a complete two-page mini paper;
- duplicate id;
- printed page 149 in a paper whose range ends at 148;
- a printed page with no unit span;
- a paragraph spanning pages 550–552 with no other unit on 551 (passes);
- a paragraph gap, a sentence gap, and a footnote gap;
- a repeated printed equation number without qualification;
- a translation unit pointing to an unknown block;
- a unit with no destination record;
- a paper marked complete with one unreviewed unit;
- a body unit whose start page precedes the previous unit's end page;
- a footnote printed on the page after its mark (passes when recorded, fails when unrecorded);
- a footnote with no mark;
- a manifest line asserting `translation: reviewed`;
- an external reference with no citation id;
- a frozen id missing without an alias (fails), and the same with a `retired` alias (passes);
- an import of an id not exported;
- an import of the importing paper's own result;
- an import from a later-received paper;
- a companion with `not-in-scope` units, with and without a whole-document completeness claim.

`src/content/manifest/report.test.ts`: per-kind and per-status counts for the fixture corpus, no percentage field, `not-in-scope` shown separately.

### End-to-end tests
Run `bun scripts/source-manifest-report.ts <fixture-slug> --json` on the fixture corpus in the quality gate chain and compare its JSON output with the expected counts; a failing fixture corpus must exit non-zero with the expected rule ids in the log.

### Structured logging
- Each failure: `{timestamp, suite: "source-manifest", runId, beadId: "am-cm-source-manifest-6qa", paper, document, unitId, rule, expected, actual, message, repair}`.
- A per-paper summary line with counts by kind, status, and scope, written to `artifacts/test-logs/content/<log-run-id>.jsonl` through the shared logger.
- The report JSON is retained under `artifacts/source-manifest/` for the coverage ledger and for failure diagnosis.

## Considerations and Pitfalls

- PDF covers or editorial matter can shift PDF page indices away from journal pages; always validate against the asset's page map.
- Footnotes, references, and date-lines are easy to forget; the inventory beads use this check as their safety net.
- Do not let `machine-draft` statuses count toward completeness.
- A naive "one unit per page" or "pages never decrease" rule rejects correct inventories; test the span and footnote cases explicitly.
- Chronology of imports is an epistemic safeguard as well as a structural one: a paper can only import what was already written.

